WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.700
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.310
YUFENG GUO: From
detecting skin cancer

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:05.010
to sorting cucumbers
to detecting escalators

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:07.170
in need of repair,
machine learning

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:10.680
has granted computer systems
entirely new abilities.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.350
But how does it really
work under the hood?

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:15.210
Let's walk through
a basic example

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:18.210
and use it as an excuse to talk
about the process of getting

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:21.470
answers from your data
using machine learning.

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:23.670
Welcome to Cloud AI Adventures.

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:25.086
My name is Yufeng Guo.

00:00:25.086 --> 00:00:27.970
On this show, we'll
explore the art, science,

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:30.972
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:30.972 --> 00:00:32.430
Let's pretend that
we've been asked

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:35.340
to create a system that answers
the question of whether a drink

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.197
is wine or beer.

00:00:37.197 --> 00:00:39.030
This question answering
system that we build

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:40.910
is called a model,
and this model

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:43.500
is created via a
process called training.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.260
In machine learning,
the goal of training

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:49.410
is to create an accurate model
that answers our questions

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.560
correctly most of the time.

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:53.400
But in order to
train the model, we

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.130
need to collect
data to train on.

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:58.350
This is where we will begin.

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:01.890
Our data will be collected
from glasses of wine and beer.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:05.069
There are many aspects of drinks
that we could collect data on--

00:01:05.069 --> 00:01:07.950
everything from the amount of
foam to the shape of the glass.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:11.250
But for our purposes, we'll
just pick two simple ones--

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:14.970
the color as a wavelength of
light and the alcohol content

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:16.730
as a percentage.

00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:19.370
The hope is that we can
split our two types of drinks

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:21.850
along these two factors alone.

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:24.110
We'll call these our
features from now on--

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:26.030
color and alcohol.

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:27.830
The first step to
our process will

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:30.032
be to run out to the
local grocery store,

00:01:30.032 --> 00:01:31.490
buy up a bunch of
different drinks,

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:34.820
and get some equipment to do our
measurements-- a spectrometer

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:36.860
for measuring the
color and a hydrometer

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:38.660
to measure the alcohol content.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:41.420
It appears that our grocery
store has an electronics

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:43.550
hardware section as well.

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:46.730
Once our equipment and then
booze-- we got it all set up--

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:48.750
it's time for our first
real step of machine

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:50.850
learning-- gathering that data.

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:53.210
This step is very important
because the quality

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:54.860
and quantity of
data that you gather

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:58.730
will directly determine how good
your predictive model can be.

00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:00.650
In this case, the
data we collect

00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:03.980
will be the color and alcohol
content of each drink.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:07.970
This will yield us a table
of color, alcohol content,

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:10.310
and whether it's beer or wine.

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:13.150
This will be our training data.

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:15.490
So a few hours of
measurements later, we've

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:19.090
gathered our training data
and had a few drinks, perhaps.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:21.910
And now it's time for our next
step of machine learning--

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:23.500
data preparation--

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:26.260
where we load our data
into a suitable place

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:30.630
and prepare it for use in our
machine learning training.

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:34.150
We'll first put all our
data together then randomize

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:35.027
the ordering.

00:02:35.027 --> 00:02:36.610
We wouldn't want the
order of our data

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:38.770
to affect how we
learn since that's not

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:41.710
part of determining whether
a drink is beer or wine.

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:44.110
In other words, we want to
make a determination of what

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:47.740
a drink is independent of what
drink came before or after it

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:49.690
in the sequence.

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:52.720
This is also a good time to do
any pertinent visualizations

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:54.990
of your data, helping
you see if there

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:58.450
is any relevant relationships
between different variables

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:01.240
as well as show you if there
are any data imbalances.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:04.420
For instance, if we collected
way more data points about beer

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:07.211
than wine, the model we
train will be heavily biased

00:03:07.211 --> 00:03:09.460
toward guessing that virtually
everything that it sees

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:12.830
is beer since it would be
right most of the time.

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:14.530
However, in the real
world, the model

00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:16.840
may see beer and wine
in equal amount, which

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:19.360
would mean that it would
be guessing beer wrong half

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:21.290
the time.

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:23.810
We also need to split
the data into two parts.

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:25.660
The first part used
in training our model

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:27.700
will be the majority
of our dataset.

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:30.940
The second part will be used
for evaluating our train model's

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:32.380
performance.

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:35.170
We don't want to use the same
data that the model was trained

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:37.870
on for evaluation since
then it would just

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:39.349
be able to memorize
the questions,

00:03:39.349 --> 00:03:41.890
just as you wouldn't want to
use the questions from your math

00:03:41.890 --> 00:03:44.380
homework on the math exam.

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:46.960
Sometimes the data we
collected needs other forms

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:49.030
of adjusting and
manipulation-- things

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:54.040
like duplication, normalization,
error correction, and others.

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:56.697
These would all happen at
the data preparation step.

00:03:56.697 --> 00:03:59.280
In our case, we don't have any
further data preparation needs,

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:01.600
so let's move on forward.

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:05.840
The next step in our
workflow is choosing a model.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:08.650
There are many models that
researchers and data scientists

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:10.880
have created over the years.

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:13.720
Some are very well suited
for image data, others

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:18.040
for sequences, such as text or
music, some for numerical data,

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:20.050
and others for text-based data.

00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:23.740
In our case, we have just two
features-- color and alcohol

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:24.820
percentage.

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:26.950
We can use a small
linear model, which

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:30.410
is a fairly simple one
that will get the job done.

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:32.710
Now we move on to what
is often considered

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:34.530
the bulk of machine learning--

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:35.950
the training.

00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:39.610
In this step, we'll use our
data to incrementally improve

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:42.250
our model's ability to
predict whether a given

00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:44.470
drink is wine or beer.

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:46.630
In some ways, this
is similar to someone

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:47.950
first learning to drive.

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:50.980
At first, they don't know
how any of the pedals, knobs,

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:54.550
and switches work or when they
should be pressed or used.

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:57.130
However, after lots of
practice and correcting

00:04:57.130 --> 00:05:00.160
for their mistakes, a
licensed driver emerges.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:02.290
Moreover, after a
year of driving,

00:05:02.290 --> 00:05:04.330
they've become quite
adept at driving.

00:05:04.330 --> 00:05:07.120
The act of driving and
reacting to real-world data

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:11.170
has adapted their driving
abilities, honing their skills.

00:05:11.170 --> 00:05:14.470
We will do this on a much
smaller scale with our drinks.

00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:16.750
In particular, the formula
for a straight line

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:20.980
is y equals mx plus b,
where x is the input,

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:23.710
m is the slope of the
line, b is the y-intercept,

00:05:23.710 --> 00:05:28.210
and y is the value of the
line at that position x.

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:31.540
The values we have available
to us to adjust or train

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:36.010
are just m and b, where the
m is that slope and b is

00:05:36.010 --> 00:05:37.090
the y-intercept.

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:41.090
There is no other way to
affect the position of the line

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:44.680
since the only other variables
are x, our input, and y,

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:45.940
our output.

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:47.890
In machine learning,
there are many m's

00:05:47.890 --> 00:05:49.900
since there may
be many features.

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:52.030
The collection of
these values is usually

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:54.130
formed into a matrix
that is denoted

00:05:54.130 --> 00:05:56.350
w for the weights matrix.

00:05:56.350 --> 00:05:58.420
Similarly, for b, we
arranged them together,

00:05:58.420 --> 00:06:01.060
and that's called the biases.

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:03.760
The training process involves
initializing some random values

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:05.800
for w and b and
attempting to predict

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:07.600
the outputs with those values.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:11.260
As you might imagine, it
does pretty poorly at first,

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:14.470
but we can compare our model's
predictions with the output

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:17.410
that it should have produced
and adjust the values in w

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:20.650
and b such that we will have
more accurate predictions

00:06:20.650 --> 00:06:22.400
on the next time around.

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:24.130
So this process then repeats.

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:27.180
Each iteration or cycle of
updating the weights and biases

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:30.071
is called one training step.

00:06:30.071 --> 00:06:32.070
So let's look at what
that means more concretely

00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:33.270
for our dataset.

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:34.920
When we first
start the training,

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:38.040
it's like we drew a random
line through the data.

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:40.530
Then as each step of
the training progresses,

00:06:40.530 --> 00:06:42.990
the line moves
step by step closer

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:46.290
to the ideal separation
of the wine and beer.

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:48.257
Once training is
complete, it's time

00:06:48.257 --> 00:06:49.590
to see if the model is any good.

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:52.590
Using evaluation, this is
where that dataset that we set

00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:54.540
aside earlier comes into play.

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:56.580
Evaluation allows
us to test our model

00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:59.640
against data that has never
been used for training.

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:01.740
This metric allows us to
see how the model might

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:04.652
perform against data
that it has not yet seen.

00:07:04.652 --> 00:07:06.360
This is meant to be
representative of how

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:09.090
the model might perform
in the real world.

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:12.840
A good rule of thumb I use for
a training-evaluation split is

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:15.930
somewhere on the order
of 80%-20% or 70%-30%.

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:19.440
Much of this depends on the size
of the original source dataset.

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:21.180
If you have a lot
of data, perhaps you

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:25.420
don't need as big of a fraction
for the evaluation dataset.

00:07:25.420 --> 00:07:27.340
Once you've done
evaluation, it's

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:29.860
possible that you want to see
if you can further improve

00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:31.300
your training in any way.

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:34.360
We can do this by tuning
some of our parameters.

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:35.865
There were a few
that we implicitly

00:07:35.865 --> 00:07:37.240
assumed when we
did our training,

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:39.550
and now is a good time
to go back and test

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:42.100
those assumptions,
try other values.

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:44.500
One example of a
parameter we can tune

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:47.344
is how many times we run
through the training set

00:07:47.344 --> 00:07:48.010
during training.

00:07:48.010 --> 00:07:50.620
We can actually show
the data multiple times.

00:07:50.620 --> 00:07:53.860
So by doing that,
we will potentially

00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:56.830
lead to higher accuracies.

00:07:56.830 --> 00:07:58.840
Another parameter
is learning rate.

00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:01.060
This defines how far
we shift the line

00:08:01.060 --> 00:08:03.250
during each step based
on the information

00:08:03.250 --> 00:08:05.350
from the previous training step.

00:08:05.350 --> 00:08:08.080
These values all play a role
in how accurate our model can

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:11.290
become and how long
the training takes.

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:13.510
For more complex models,
initial conditions

00:08:13.510 --> 00:08:15.760
can play a significant
role as well in determining

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:17.380
the outcome of training.

00:08:17.380 --> 00:08:18.760
Differences can
be seen depending

00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:20.380
on whether a model
starts off training

00:08:20.380 --> 00:08:23.680
with values initialized at
zeros versus some distribution

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:26.455
of the values and what
that distribution is.

00:08:26.455 --> 00:08:28.330
As you can see, there
are many considerations

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:30.205
at this phase of training,
and it's important

00:08:30.205 --> 00:08:33.320
that you define what makes
a model good enough for you.

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:36.250
Otherwise, we might find
ourselves tweaking parameters

00:08:36.250 --> 00:08:38.260
for a very long time.

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:39.760
Now, these parameters
are typically

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:42.610
referred to as hyperparameters.

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:45.040
The adjustment or tuning
of these hyperparameters

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:48.760
still remains a bit more
of an art than a science,

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:50.890
and it's an experimental
process that

00:08:50.890 --> 00:08:54.420
heavily depends on the specifics
of your dataset, model,

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:56.350
and training process.

00:08:56.350 --> 00:08:58.840
Once you're happy with your
training and hyperparameters,

00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:00.790
guided by the
evaluation step, it's

00:09:00.790 --> 00:09:04.000
finally time to use your
model to do something useful.

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:06.520
Machine learning is using
data to answer questions,

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:10.060
so prediction or inference
is that step where we finally

00:09:10.060 --> 00:09:12.020
get to answer some questions.

00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:15.160
This is the point of all of this
work where the value of machine

00:09:15.160 --> 00:09:17.320
learning is realized.

00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:19.630
We can finally use our model
to predict whether a given

00:09:19.630 --> 00:09:24.260
drink is wine or beer, given its
color and alcohol percentage.

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:25.930
The power of machine
learning is that we

00:09:25.930 --> 00:09:30.010
were able to determine how
to differentiate between wine

00:09:30.010 --> 00:09:32.965
and beer using our model rather
than using human judgment

00:09:32.965 --> 00:09:34.620
and manual rules.

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:36.800
You can extrapolate the
ideas presented today

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:38.770
to other problem
domains as well, where

00:09:38.770 --> 00:09:40.570
the same principles apply--

00:09:40.570 --> 00:09:44.590
gathering data, preparing
that data, choosing a model,

00:09:44.590 --> 00:09:47.620
training it and evaluating
it, doing your hyperparameter

00:09:47.620 --> 00:09:50.286
training, and
finally, prediction.

00:09:50.286 --> 00:09:51.910
If you're looking
for more ways to play

00:09:51.910 --> 00:09:53.650
with training and
parameters, check out

00:09:53.650 --> 00:09:55.280
the TensorFlow Playground.

00:09:55.280 --> 00:09:57.940
It's a completely browser-based
machine learning sandbox,

00:09:57.940 --> 00:09:59.740
where you can try
different parameters

00:09:59.740 --> 00:10:02.410
and run training
against mock datasets.

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:04.960
And don't worry, you
can't break the site.

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:07.720
Of course, we will encounter
more steps and nuances

00:10:07.720 --> 00:10:09.580
in future episodes,
but this serves

00:10:09.580 --> 00:10:11.410
as a good foundational
framework to help

00:10:11.410 --> 00:10:14.310
us think through the problem,
giving us a common language

00:10:14.310 --> 00:10:17.240
to think about each step
and go deeper in the future.

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:19.030
Next time on AI
Adventures, we'll

00:10:19.030 --> 00:10:22.600
build our first real machine
learning model, using code--

00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:24.970
no more drawing lines
and going over algebra.

00:10:24.970 --> 00:10:27.420
[MUSIC PLAYING]

